[165.247173, "o", "    \u001b[2mUsage: mailman withlist [OPTIONS] [RUN_ARGS]...\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m  Operate on a mailing list.\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m  For detailed help, see --details\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2mOptions:\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m  -i, --interactive    Leaves you at an interactive prompt after all other\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m                       processing is complete. This is the default unless the\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m                       --run option is given.\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m\u001b[3m... (11 lines hidden, /toggle to show all)\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m                       This may be the posting address of the list, or its\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m                       List-ID.  The argument can also be a Python regular\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m                       expression, in which case it is matched against both\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m                       the posting address and List-ID of all mailing lists.\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m                       To use a regular expression, LISTSPEC must start with a\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m                       ^ (and the matching is done with re.match(). LISTSPEC\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m                       cannot be a regular expression unless --run is given.\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m  -h, --help           Show this message and exit.\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2m\u001b[0m\r\n    \u001b[2mCommand exited with code 1\u001b[0m\r\n"]
